@jhamburger

I think your issue is not related to this.

Notice how your python script doesn't close with ```. You should check your max tokens to ensure that you're not being capped causing premature responses that aren't complete. You have incomplete code there, and without a closing code block fence, The code extractor doesn't match.

I will summarize the reply here since it would be too long to reply one by one:

@afourney About group chat: It is not addressed here and should be the next thing to do. Some ideas:
1. From this compressible agent, an idea is to broadcast the messages to all agents. Current this PR allows broadcast to sender, we can add another level of broadcast to all agents in self.oai_messages and sender.oai_messages and etc. The group chat manager can be made a compressible agent and responsible for compression. 
2. A separate compression module in group manager
Again, it is a complex scenario that needs more discussion.

@AaronWard thank you for your detailed code reviews and I will revise them later!

@afourney @gagb @rickyloynd-microsoft @sonichi 
The purpose of this PR is to provide a structure for adding compression and some usable modes to start with. We can add more modes in the compressible agent later for different scenarios if proven useful. The current way of compression is not perfect and it is hard to have a perfect one for all scenarios. For some tasks you can compress all the messages directly and for some tasks you may need to save certain content (e.g. save all function calls).

Thanks @rickyloynd-microsoft for the suggestion, I agree use TERMINATE as default is better and I will change that. I am aware of the sliding window idea (#195), but I didn't add it since this PR is already long and a good implementation of the method require more effort and time. The third example in the notebook uses a simple sliding window method, and demonstrates that the agent allows customized compress functions for more flexibility. I think we can leave the option to users for now.

@g7xcy there is increasing interest on this issue. I think it would be great if you could revise this PR to subclass the GroupChat to ensure a fixed order.

I haven't delved too deeply into the code at this stage, but I think if you just assume a chat order that matches the order of agents provided in the GroupChat constructor, and override select_speaker to call next_agent, this should be enough. You could then pass an instance of this modified class to the GroupChatManager and get the behavior we want.

The CustomGroupChat class in this notebook is a good example: https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/blob/4e0a8972958a955acda65336494f9e02a2fb681f/notebook/agentchat_hierarchy_flow_using_select_speaker.ipynb

